 RGBXY Derivatives with Optimal Transport (DROT) is a differentiable rendering method that associates each screen-space pixel with a visible 3D geometric point (point proxy) to compute 5D RGBXY derivatives, measuring how 3D RGB color and 2D projected screen-space position change with respect to scene parameters. It combines these dense derivatives with an optimal transport-based loss function to find pixel matching between rendered and target images, solving the problem of converging in inverse rendering when initial and target object locations are not close. Unlike traditional methods that rely on sparse, local per-pixel color derivatives and L2 loss, DROT captures global and long-range object motions, making it superior for optimizing global translations and rotations.
 
+### [Sensitivity AOV](10809-sensitivity-aov.md)
+A render output carrying the sensitivity of a scalar objective M with respect to scene parameters θ. It allows human inspection of which parts of a scene matter for a given goal, analogous to how primal AOVs expose intermediate light transport quantities. Not given in the sources.
+
+### [Generalized Differentiable Renderer (GenDR)](11684-generalized-differentiable-renderer-gendr.md)
+A generalized differentiable mesh renderer that models the forward rendering process under a probabilistic perturbation defined by a probability distribution with density f and a temperature/scale parameter τ > 0, making the renderer continuous everywhere and differentiable almost everywhere. Not given in the sources.
+
 ### Differentiable Monte Carlo Ray Tracing
 A rendering technique used to compute global illumination via single-bounce differentiable MC ray tracing within the JOC framework [S2, unofficial]. JOC uses single-bounce differentiable MC rendering, whereas previous methods like NVDIFFRECMC avoided multi-bounce due to noise and cost, limiting them to direct lighting [S2, unofficial].
